;; FH-TOPLAYER-005 — the top layer's development advisories: WHEN they are
;; published, and WHAT they are.
;;
;; Two advisories, one seam. Neither is about a value the tree carries, so
;; neither is provable structurally; both are about a moment.
;;
;;   1. WHEN. An advisory is evidence, and evidence is published only from a
;;      SELECTED commit — the same passive-render law the reactive bundle
;;      obeys. Constructing an element's props is not a commit: React may
;;      restart or abandon that render, and an advisory published from one
;;      names a candidate that never existed, accusing an author of a mistake
;;      the page never made. So the publication rides the callback ref, which
;;      runs only for a render the host committed, and building props publishes
;;      exactly ZERO.
;;
;;   2. WHAT. Both advisories carry a stable, catalogued diagnostic id on the
;;      trace DIAGNOSTIC channel, with the fields a reader needs: the
;;      mechanism and the handlers that reconcile it for an unreconciled
;;      declaration; the host call, the element, and the browser's own reason
;;      for a refused one — each with a `:recovery`. Unstructured console prose
;;      is not evidence a tool can read, and scraping it is not a contract.
;;
;; The refusal case is a REAL browser refusal, not a stub: a <dialog> already
;; open non-modally cannot be promoted with showModal(), which is exactly the
;; authoring mistake the advisory names. The runtime continues afterwards —
;; the operation is mechanical and the next render can fix it, so taking the
;; page down would be the wrong trade.
;;
;; Both are DEV-only: `interop/debug-enabled?` gates the pair, so `:advanced`
;; + goog.DEBUG=false strips them entirely.
{:fh/id "FH-TOPLAYER-005"
 :fh/law
 "Top-layer development advisories are published only from a SELECTED commit — constructing props publishes zero — and each is typed dev evidence carrying a stable catalogued diagnostic id and a recovery on the trace diagnostic channel: an unreconciled controlled declaration names its mechanism and the handlers that reconcile it, and a refused host call names the call, the element and the browser's reason before the runtime continues."

 :unreconciled-id :rf.warning/view-top-layer-unreconciled
 :refused-id      :rf.warning/view-top-layer-refused
 :recovery        :warned-and-continued

 :menu-id   "advisory-menu"
 :dialog-id "advisory-dialog"}
